  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-Atlantic \nand low pressure well off the FL coast will result in a period \nof continued long period swell across the OBX. The high waves \ncombined with King Tides this week will lead to additional minor\ncoastal flooding impacts.\n\nFor Hatteras Island, seas offshore are in the 4-7 foot range\nearly this morning and continuing to subside, but the long \nperiod swells will continue to cause issues for vulnerable \nareas. For ocean- side: high tide is around 0900 EDT today. \nSoundside high tides lag by up to 3 hours behind the oceanside \ntides. The National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported \nsubstantial beach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along \nwith significant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe.\nDue to hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains \nclosed from the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. \n\nCoastal flooding will be likely well into this week despite the\ndiminishing of winds, with \"sunny day\" flooding across much of \nthe tidal regions of ENC around times of high tide, then slowly\nimprove as the King Tides ease later in the week and into the\nweekend. Coastal flood advisories continue through the high tide\ncycle on Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry high pres with near climo temps expected\ntoday and tonight, then warming to above normal mid week through\nthe weekend. After a chilly start today with lows in the upper\n30s to low 40s for most of us, temps rapidly warm today with\nbuilding hts/thicknesses and highs in the mid 70s inland to\nupper 60s OBX. Normal highs are in the low 70s. Patchy fog,\nlocally dense, has formed and will be with us through this\nmorning's commute, with the highest concentration across coastal\ncommunties where dewpoints are highest. \n\nMid week into the weekend, hts/thicknesses build further, and temps\nclimbing back to 5-10 degrees above climo with highs 75-80\ninland to low 70s OBX. Low temps will warm above climo as well \nwith readings in the mid 50s for most locales away from the \nimmediate coast.",
